1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,248
Sweetheart, shepherd's pie is the most comforting

2
00:00:03,248 --> 00:00:04,574
supper in the world.

3
00:00:04,754 --> 00:00:07,903
A rich, meaty filling in thick gravy,

4
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:11,392
under a fluffy blanket of mashed potatoes

5
00:00:11,392 --> 00:00:12,583
with a golden,

6
00:00:12,583 --> 00:00:13,519
crispy top.

7
00:00:13,699 --> 00:00:16,060
But most of the ones I've eaten

8
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:17,659
are soupy underneath,

9
00:00:17,659 --> 00:00:20,704
with the potatoes sinking into a puddle,

10
00:00:20,704 --> 00:00:23,521
or dry and bland like cafeteria food.

11
00:00:23,701 --> 00:00:26,883
And it's always the same five mistakes.

12
00:00:27,063 --> 00:00:29,280
Every one is easy to fix.

13
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:32,357
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

14
00:00:32,357 --> 00:00:34,494
shepherd's pie, step by step,

15
00:00:34,494 --> 00:00:37,369
the Monday supper that used up Sunday's

16
00:00:37,369 --> 00:00:39,138
leftovers on Elm Street.

17
00:00:39,318 --> 00:00:40,497
And stay with me,

18
00:00:40,497 --> 00:00:42,924
because the last secret is what she

19
00:00:42,924 --> 00:00:45,004
did to the top of the potatoes

20
00:00:45,004 --> 00:00:46,738
with a plain dinner fork,

21
00:00:46,738 --> 00:00:48,749
and it's why the crust was so

22
00:00:48,749 --> 00:00:51,176
crispy that Danny and I fought over

23
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:52,008
the corners.

24
00:00:52,358 --> 00:00:53,496
In our house,

25
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:55,335
Sunday was roast day,

26
00:00:55,335 --> 00:00:58,313
and Monday was shepherd's pie day.

27
00:00:58,493 --> 00:01:01,045
My mother took whatever was left of

28
00:01:01,045 --> 00:01:03,450
Sunday's pot roast or roast beef,

29
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:04,835
chopped it up fine,

30
00:01:04,835 --> 00:01:07,460
mixed it with the leftover gravy and

31
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:10,667
vegetables, and covered it with the leftover

32
00:01:10,667 --> 00:01:11,833
mashed potatoes.

33
00:01:12,013 --> 00:01:14,740
Nothing was ever wasted in her kitchen,

34
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:15,160
honey.

35
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:17,917
Sunday dinner fed us twice.

36
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:20,071
That was the whole point of it.

37
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:23,298
It came from the farmhouses of England

38
00:01:23,298 --> 00:01:26,327
and Ireland, where poor families did the

39
00:01:26,327 --> 00:01:28,751
same thing with leftover mutton,

40
00:01:28,751 --> 00:01:30,417
two hundred years ago.

41
00:01:30,597 --> 00:01:32,598
When it was made with lamb,

42
00:01:32,598 --> 00:01:34,821
they called it shepherd's pie,

43
00:01:34,821 --> 00:01:36,971
because shepherds tend sheep.

44
00:01:37,151 --> 00:01:39,393
When it was made with beef,

45
00:01:39,393 --> 00:01:40,970
it was cottage pie.

46
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:44,459
In America, nearly everybody makes it with

47
00:01:44,459 --> 00:01:47,611
ground beef, and calls it shepherd's pie

48
00:01:47,611 --> 00:01:48,163
anyway.

49
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:50,622
In a minute I'll tell you which

50
00:01:50,622 --> 00:01:51,431
one I make.

51
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:54,217
My father loved it so much that

52
00:01:54,217 --> 00:01:56,733
when my mother asked him what he

53
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:59,877
wanted for his fiftieth birthday dinner,

54
00:01:59,877 --> 00:02:01,763
he said, shepherd's pie,

55
00:02:01,763 --> 00:02:02,392
Dorothy.

56
00:02:02,572 --> 00:02:03,909
She said, Walter,

57
00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:06,267
I was going to make a standing

58
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:07,053
rib roast.

59
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:08,200
And he said,

60
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,615
then make the roast on Sunday,

61
00:02:10,615 --> 00:02:12,386
and the pie on Monday,

62
00:02:12,386 --> 00:02:14,641
and I'll have two birthdays.

63
00:02:14,821 --> 00:02:17,456
And that's exactly what she did.

64
00:02:17,806 --> 00:02:20,402
Here's what you need for a nine

65
00:02:20,402 --> 00:02:21,742
by thirteen pan.

66
00:02:21,922 --> 00:02:23,098
For the filling,

67
00:02:23,098 --> 00:02:25,375
one and a half pounds of ground

68
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:26,845
beef or ground lamb.

69
00:02:27,025 --> 00:02:29,148
One onion, two carrots,

70
00:02:29,148 --> 00:02:31,087
two stalks of celery,

71
00:02:31,087 --> 00:02:32,749
all chopped small.

72
00:02:32,929 --> 00:02:34,775
Two cloves of garlic.

73
00:02:34,955 --> 00:02:38,034
Two tablespoons of tomato paste and two

74
00:02:38,034 --> 00:02:39,691
tablespoons of flour.

75
00:02:39,871 --> 00:02:41,684
One cup of beef broth,

76
00:02:41,684 --> 00:02:44,731
a tablespoon of Worcestershire sauce,

77
00:02:44,731 --> 00:02:47,285
some thyme, and a cup of frozen

78
00:02:47,285 --> 00:02:47,697
peas.

79
00:02:48,046 --> 00:02:49,023
For the top,

80
00:02:49,023 --> 00:02:51,544
two and a half pounds of russet

81
00:02:51,544 --> 00:02:54,228
potatoes, half a stick of butter,

82
00:02:54,228 --> 00:02:56,180
half a cup of warm milk,

83
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:57,481
salt and pepper,

84
00:02:57,481 --> 00:02:58,864
and one egg yolk,

85
00:02:58,864 --> 00:03:01,467
which is my mother's trick for a

86
00:03:01,467 --> 00:03:02,362
golden top.

87
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:05,006
And a handful of sharp cheddar,

88
00:03:05,006 --> 00:03:06,199
if you like it,

89
00:03:06,199 --> 00:03:07,789
which my father did.

90
00:03:07,969 --> 00:03:11,801
Russets, honey, not red potatoes.

91
00:03:11,981 --> 00:03:14,825
Russets mash up light and fluffy.

92
00:03:15,005 --> 00:03:17,443
Red potatoes turn gluey.

93
00:03:17,793 --> 00:03:20,262
Brown the meat in a big skillet

94
00:03:20,262 --> 00:03:22,014
over medium-high heat,

95
00:03:22,014 --> 00:03:24,244
breaking it up with a spoon,

96
00:03:24,244 --> 00:03:26,156
until it's well browned,

97
00:03:26,156 --> 00:03:26,872
not gray.

98
00:03:27,052 --> 00:03:29,421
And here's mistake number one.

99
00:03:29,601 --> 00:03:31,319
Not draining the fat.

100
00:03:31,499 --> 00:03:33,698
If you leave all that grease in,

101
00:03:33,698 --> 00:03:35,690
it pools at the bottom of the

102
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:38,026
pan and makes the whole pie greasy

103
00:03:38,026 --> 00:03:39,882
and the potatoes slide off.

104
00:03:40,062 --> 00:03:42,296
Tip the pan and spoon most of

105
00:03:42,296 --> 00:03:45,071
it out, leaving just a tablespoon or

106
00:03:45,071 --> 00:03:45,379
two.

107
00:03:45,728 --> 00:03:46,817
Add the onion,

108
00:03:46,817 --> 00:03:48,294
carrots and celery,

109
00:03:48,294 --> 00:03:50,548
and cook them in the same pan

110
00:03:50,548 --> 00:03:52,413
for about eight minutes,

111
00:03:52,413 --> 00:03:53,890
until they're soft.

112
00:03:54,070 --> 00:03:56,554
Then the garlic and tomato paste,

113
00:03:56,554 --> 00:03:57,758
for two minutes,

114
00:03:57,758 --> 00:04:00,316
stirring, until the paste darkens.

115
00:04:00,496 --> 00:04:03,736
Sprinkle the flour over everything and stir

116
00:04:03,736 --> 00:04:04,188
it in.

117
00:04:04,368 --> 00:04:07,399
That flour is what thickens the gravy.

118
00:04:07,579 --> 00:04:10,080
Mistake number two is skipping it,

119
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:12,582
and that's exactly how you get the

120
00:04:12,582 --> 00:04:14,347
soupy puddle underneath.

121
00:04:14,697 --> 00:04:16,501
If this is helping you,

122
00:04:16,501 --> 00:04:18,618
tap the like button for me,

123
00:04:18,618 --> 00:04:19,481
sweetheart.

124
00:04:19,661 --> 00:04:22,378
It helps Grandma more than you know.

125
00:04:22,558 --> 00:04:25,532
Pour in the broth and the Worcestershire

126
00:04:25,532 --> 00:04:27,094
sauce, add the thyme,

127
00:04:27,094 --> 00:04:29,177
and let it simmer for ten to

128
00:04:29,177 --> 00:04:31,705
fifteen minutes, until it's thick,

129
00:04:31,705 --> 00:04:32,449
like stew.

130
00:04:32,629 --> 00:04:33,813
Not soup.

131
00:04:33,993 --> 00:04:36,731
Drag a spoon across the bottom of

132
00:04:36,731 --> 00:04:37,395
the pan.

133
00:04:37,575 --> 00:04:39,901
If the gravy doesn't rush back in

134
00:04:39,901 --> 00:04:41,874
to fill the line right away,

135
00:04:41,874 --> 00:04:42,649
it's ready.

136
00:04:42,829 --> 00:04:44,837
Stir in the frozen peas.

137
00:04:45,187 --> 00:04:47,463
And mistake number three.

138
00:04:47,643 --> 00:04:50,854
Putting hot potatoes on hot filling straight

139
00:04:50,854 --> 00:04:51,219
away.

140
00:04:51,399 --> 00:04:54,136
Let the filling cool for ten minutes

141
00:04:54,136 --> 00:04:56,113
first, in the baking dish.

142
00:04:56,293 --> 00:04:57,962
If it's bubbling hot,

143
00:04:57,962 --> 00:05:00,745
the potatoes sink right into it and

144
00:05:00,745 --> 00:05:01,540
disappear.

145
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:05,320
A slightly cooled filling holds the potatoes

146
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:06,138
up on top,

147
00:05:06,138 --> 00:05:07,038
like a lid.

148
00:05:07,218 --> 00:05:09,869
My mother made the filling first,

149
00:05:09,869 --> 00:05:11,316
then the potatoes,

150
00:05:11,316 --> 00:05:13,324
so it had time to settle.

151
00:05:13,674 --> 00:05:16,158
And here's a little trick from the

152
00:05:16,158 --> 00:05:17,912
nineteen fifties, honey,

153
00:05:17,912 --> 00:05:19,665
when meat was expensive.

154
00:05:19,845 --> 00:05:21,857
Stretch the filling with a cup of

155
00:05:21,857 --> 00:05:23,929
cooked lentils or a can of drained

156
00:05:23,929 --> 00:05:25,697
beans mixed in with the beef.

157
00:05:25,877 --> 00:05:27,777
It feeds two more people,

158
00:05:27,777 --> 00:05:28,993
nobody can tell,

159
00:05:28,993 --> 00:05:31,577
and it makes the filling heartier.

160
00:05:31,757 --> 00:05:34,043
My mother did that all through the

161
00:05:34,043 --> 00:05:36,598
lean years when my father's hours were

162
00:05:36,598 --> 00:05:39,153
cut, and my father never once noticed.

163
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:40,473
Or if he did,

164
00:05:40,473 --> 00:05:41,701
he never said.

165
00:05:42,051 --> 00:05:44,689
Peel and cut the potatoes into even

166
00:05:44,689 --> 00:05:46,799
chunks, put them in a pot of

167
00:05:46,799 --> 00:05:48,155
cold salted water,

168
00:05:48,155 --> 00:05:49,888
and bring it to a boil.

169
00:05:50,068 --> 00:05:52,088
Cook about fifteen minutes,

170
00:05:52,088 --> 00:05:54,631
until a fork slides right through.

171
00:05:54,811 --> 00:05:55,928
Drain them well,

172
00:05:55,928 --> 00:05:57,954
then put them back in the hot

173
00:05:57,954 --> 00:06:00,259
pot over low heat for one minute,

174
00:06:00,259 --> 00:06:02,564
shaking it, to steam off the last

175
00:06:02,564 --> 00:06:03,472
of the water.

176
00:06:03,652 --> 00:06:06,303
Wet potatoes make a soggy top,

177
00:06:06,303 --> 00:06:06,833
honey.

178
00:06:07,183 --> 00:06:09,319
Mash them with the butter,

179
00:06:09,319 --> 00:06:11,537
warm milk, salt and pepper.

180
00:06:11,717 --> 00:06:14,213
And here's mistake number four.

181
00:06:14,393 --> 00:06:16,158
Making them too soft.

182
00:06:16,338 --> 00:06:17,549
For shepherd's pie,

183
00:06:17,549 --> 00:06:19,970
you want the potatoes a little stiffer

184
00:06:19,970 --> 00:06:22,073
than you'd serve them on a plate.

185
00:06:22,253 --> 00:06:25,841
Soft, runny potatoes melt into the filling.

186
00:06:26,021 --> 00:06:27,995
Then beat in that egg yolk.

187
00:06:28,175 --> 00:06:30,647
It makes them richer and helps them

188
00:06:30,647 --> 00:06:32,343
turn golden in the oven.

189
00:06:32,523 --> 00:06:33,742
Mother's trick.

190
00:06:34,091 --> 00:06:36,263
Heat your oven to four hundred.

191
00:06:36,443 --> 00:06:38,682
Now here's how to put the potatoes

192
00:06:38,682 --> 00:06:40,262
on without them sinking.

193
00:06:40,442 --> 00:06:42,674
Don't dump them in the middle and

194
00:06:42,674 --> 00:06:43,147
spread.

195
00:06:43,327 --> 00:06:46,183
Drop big spoonfuls all around the edges

196
00:06:46,183 --> 00:06:48,453
first, then fill in the middle,

197
00:06:48,453 --> 00:06:51,455
then gently spread them together with the

198
00:06:51,455 --> 00:06:52,627
back of a spoon.

199
00:06:52,807 --> 00:06:54,954
Seal them right up to the edges

200
00:06:54,954 --> 00:06:55,784
of the dish,

201
00:06:55,784 --> 00:06:58,070
so the gravy can't bubble up over

202
00:06:58,070 --> 00:06:58,624
the top.

203
00:06:58,973 --> 00:07:01,196
And put the dish on a baking

204
00:07:01,196 --> 00:07:03,815
sheet before it goes in the oven,

205
00:07:03,815 --> 00:07:04,291
honey.

206
00:07:04,471 --> 00:07:07,027
Shepherd's pie loves to bubble over,

207
00:07:07,027 --> 00:07:09,299
and burnt gravy on the bottom of

208
00:07:09,299 --> 00:07:11,855
your oven smokes up the whole house.

209
00:07:12,035 --> 00:07:13,916
Ask me how I know.

210
00:07:14,096 --> 00:07:17,032
Harold once called the fire department over

211
00:07:17,032 --> 00:07:19,831
a shepherd's pie in nineteen seventy-six.

212
00:07:20,011 --> 00:07:22,495
The firemen stayed for supper.

213
00:07:22,845 --> 00:07:25,593
Now the secret I promised you,

214
00:07:25,593 --> 00:07:27,790
and mistake number five,

215
00:07:27,790 --> 00:07:29,347
a flat, pale top.

216
00:07:29,527 --> 00:07:31,931
Take a plain dinner fork and drag

217
00:07:31,931 --> 00:07:34,263
it across the potatoes in lines,

218
00:07:34,263 --> 00:07:35,647
all the way across,

219
00:07:35,647 --> 00:07:36,740
back and forth,

220
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:38,124
then the other way,

221
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:39,581
like a plowed field.

222
00:07:39,761 --> 00:07:42,838
Make lots of little ridges and peaks.

223
00:07:43,018 --> 00:07:45,473
Brush the top with a little melted

224
00:07:45,473 --> 00:07:45,979
butter.

225
00:07:46,159 --> 00:07:47,118
In the oven,

226
00:07:47,118 --> 00:07:50,077
every one of those ridges turns brown

227
00:07:50,077 --> 00:07:52,556
and crispy, like a potato chip.

228
00:07:52,905 --> 00:07:54,651
Bake for twenty-five minutes,

229
00:07:54,651 --> 00:07:57,180
until the filling bubbles around the edges

230
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:58,745
and the ridges are golden.

231
00:07:58,925 --> 00:08:00,415
If you want them darker,

232
00:08:00,415 --> 00:08:02,402
turn on the broiler for the last

233
00:08:02,402 --> 00:08:04,327
two minutes and watch it like a

234
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:04,637
hawk.

235
00:08:04,817 --> 00:08:06,651
Then, and this is hard,

236
00:08:06,651 --> 00:08:09,362
let it rest for ten minutes before

237
00:08:09,362 --> 00:08:10,239
you cut it.

238
00:08:10,419 --> 00:08:11,273
It firms up,

239
00:08:11,273 --> 00:08:13,690
and you get a clean square instead

240
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:14,401
of a mess.

241
00:08:14,581 --> 00:08:16,567
Those corners, honey.

242
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:18,813
Those crispy corners.

243
00:08:19,163 --> 00:08:20,557
Once you know how,

244
00:08:20,557 --> 00:08:22,646
change it any way you like.

245
00:08:22,826 --> 00:08:25,213
A layer of corn under the potatoes,

246
00:08:25,213 --> 00:08:27,123
which is how they make it in

247
00:08:27,123 --> 00:08:29,305
Quebec and call it pâté chinois.

248
00:08:29,485 --> 00:08:32,341
Sweet potatoes on top instead of white.

249
00:08:32,521 --> 00:08:34,413
Cheddar mixed into the mash.

250
00:08:34,593 --> 00:08:36,854
A splash of red wine in the

251
00:08:36,854 --> 00:08:37,357
gravy.

252
00:08:37,537 --> 00:08:40,987
Or use up leftover Thanksgiving turkey,

253
00:08:40,987 --> 00:08:43,287
gravy and mashed potatoes,

254
00:08:43,287 --> 00:08:44,526
the day after.

255
00:08:44,706 --> 00:08:46,424
That's my favorite of all.

256
00:08:46,774 --> 00:08:48,732
It freezes beautifully,

257
00:08:48,732 --> 00:08:50,094
too, sweetheart.

258
00:08:50,274 --> 00:08:51,867
Make two, bake one,

259
00:08:51,867 --> 00:08:54,803
and freeze the other before baking,

260
00:08:54,803 --> 00:08:56,648
wrapped tight in foil.

261
00:08:56,828 --> 00:08:57,899
On a busy night,

262
00:08:57,899 --> 00:09:00,378
put it straight from the freezer into

263
00:09:00,378 --> 00:09:03,324
a three hundred seventy-five degree oven for

264
00:09:03,324 --> 00:09:05,200
an hour and fifteen minutes,

265
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:07,209
with the foil on for the first

266
00:09:07,209 --> 00:09:07,544
hour.

267
00:09:07,724 --> 00:09:10,887
Supper's ready, and you didn't lift a

268
00:09:10,887 --> 00:09:11,485
finger.

269
00:09:11,836 --> 00:09:14,645
Serve it with something green and sharp

270
00:09:14,645 --> 00:09:15,509
on the side,

271
00:09:15,509 --> 00:09:17,815
sweetheart, to cut the richness.

272
00:09:17,995 --> 00:09:20,876
A simple salad with vinegar dressing,

273
00:09:20,876 --> 00:09:22,200
or pickled beets,

274
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,082
or a little bowl of chow-chow relish,

275
00:09:25,082 --> 00:09:27,341
which is what we had in Ohio.

276
00:09:27,521 --> 00:09:29,832
And a big glass of cold milk

277
00:09:29,832 --> 00:09:31,236
for the children.

278
00:09:31,416 --> 00:09:33,892
My father liked a bottle of ketchup

279
00:09:33,892 --> 00:09:34,812
on the table,

280
00:09:34,812 --> 00:09:37,218
and my mother pretended not to see

281
00:09:37,218 --> 00:09:37,430
it.

282
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:39,300
So one more time.

283
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:42,220
Russet potatoes, not red.

284
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:45,001
Brown the meat and drain the fat.

285
00:09:45,181 --> 00:09:46,655
Don't skip the flour,

286
00:09:46,655 --> 00:09:49,251
and cook the filling until it's thick

287
00:09:49,251 --> 00:09:49,953
like stew.

288
00:09:50,133 --> 00:09:52,682
Let it cool before the potatoes go

289
00:09:52,682 --> 00:09:52,907
on.

290
00:09:53,087 --> 00:09:55,009
Make them a little stiff,

291
00:09:55,009 --> 00:09:56,315
with an egg yolk.

292
00:09:56,495 --> 00:09:58,004
Edges first.

293
00:09:58,184 --> 00:10:00,165
And plow the top with a fork

294
00:10:00,165 --> 00:10:01,934
and brush it with butter.

295
00:10:02,114 --> 00:10:04,470
That's my mother's Monday supper,

296
00:10:04,470 --> 00:10:06,825
and it's the best thing that ever

297
00:10:06,825 --> 00:10:08,395
happened to leftovers.

298
00:10:08,745 --> 00:10:10,680
Tell me in the comments,

299
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:11,567
sweetheart.

300
00:10:11,747 --> 00:10:14,719
Beef or lamb in your shepherd's pie?

301
00:10:14,899 --> 00:10:16,991
And do you fight over the corners

302
00:10:16,991 --> 00:10:17,244
too?

303
00:10:17,424 --> 00:10:20,473
The whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

304
00:10:20,473 --> 00:10:22,433
right there on your screen,

305
00:10:22,433 --> 00:10:24,465
and I'll see you in the next

306
00:10:24,465 --> 00:10:24,901
video.

307
00:10:25,081 --> 00:10:26,753
Good night, honey.
